   "text": "## Review Comments\n\n**1. SQL injection in `display_draw_image()` \u2014 High**\n```php\n$filename = $base_name.\".jpg\";\n$sql = \"SELECT * from documents where documents.url like '%\".$filename.\"'\";\n$doc = sqlQuery($sql);\n```\n`$base_name` is built from `$pid` and `$encounter`, which ultimately come from `$_REQUEST['ptid']`/`$_REQUEST['encid']` with no casting/sanitization. This query is **not** parameterized (unlike nearly every other query in the file), so it's a straightforward SQL-injection vector. Should use `sqlQuery($sql, array($like_param))`.\n\n**2. SQL injection risk building facility query \u2014 Medium**\n```php\n$sql = \"select * from facility where id=\" . $_SESSION['pc_facility'];\n```\nSession value concatenated directly into SQL rather than parameterized, inconsistent with the rest of the file's style. Should be `sqlQuery(\"select * from facility where id=?\", array($_SESSION['pc_facility']))`.\n\n**3. Inconsistent output escaping \u2192 stored XSS \u2014 High**\nSeveral fields pulled straight from patient/encounter data are echoed without `text()`/escaping, while sibling fields a few lines later are properly escaped:\n```php\n<b><?php echo xlt('HPI'); ?>:</b>   \n&nbsp;<?php echo $HPI1; ?>   // not escaped, unlike $HPI2/$HPI3 later\n...\necho $item['codetext'].\"<br />\";      // IMPPLAN codetext, raw\necho  $item['plan'].\"</div><br />\";   // IMPPLAN plan text, raw\n...\necho $item.\"<br />\";                  // $PLAN_items, raw\n...\n$providerNAME.\"<br />                 // raw in the PDF signature block\n```\nSince these are user-entered clinical text fields, this is a real stored-XSS exposure when rendered outside the PDF/offline context.\n\n**4. No apparent authorization/IDOR check on `$pid`/`$encounter` \u2014 High**\n`ptid`/`encid`/`formid` are taken straight from `$_REQUEST` and used to pull and render a full clinical report with no visible check that the current user/session is actually permitted to view that patient/encounter (e.g. ACL/facility/care-team check). Any authenticated user could potentially view another patient's exam by changing the query string.\n\n**5. Logic bug in choice handling \u2014 Medium**\n```php\nif ($_REQUEST['CHOICE']) $choice = $_REQUEST['choice'];\n```\nChecks `CHOICE` (uppercase) but assigns from `choice` (lowercase) \u2014 PHP array keys are case-sensitive, so this condition will essentially never be satisfied via a normal `choice=` parameter, silently breaking the \"choice\" feature described in the comments above it.\n\n**6. Use of short open tag `<?` instead of `<?php` \u2014 High (reliability)**\n```php\n                    </div>\n                    <?\n                  }\n```\nIf `short_open_tag` is off (the default since PHP 5.4+), this is treated as literal text rather than PHP, meaning the closing `}` for the `if ($CC2)` block is never actually parsed as code. Depending on server config this can produce broken output or a fatal parse error for the whole script. All PHP tags should be `<?php`.\n\n**7. Unsafe dynamic variable creation from DB values \u2014 Medium**\n```php\n$LOCATION = $prefs['LOCATION'];\n$$LOCATION = text($prefs['GOVALUE']);\n...\n@extract($objQuery);\n@extract($encounter_data);\n```\nVariable-variable assignment and `extract()` driven by database content can silently clobber unrelated variables (including ones used for access control later, like `$pid`/`$encounter`), and the `@` suppresses any warnings that would reveal collisions. This is fragile and hard to audit; prefer explicit field-to-variable mapping.\n\n**8. Copy/paste bug in motility table (OS inferior-oblique cell) \u2014 Low/Medium (correctness)**\n```php\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"mot\"><?php echo $MOTILITY_LLIO; ?></td>\n  <td class=\"mot\"><?php echo $MOTILITY_LI; ?></td>\n  <td class=\"mot\"><?php echo $MOTILITY_LLIO; ?></td>\n</tr>\n```\nFirst column should be `$MOTILITY_LRIO` (mirroring the OD block's `RRIO/RI/RLIO` pattern); `$MOTILITY_LLIO` is duplicated, so the left-superior-oblique... actually right-position cell will always show the wrong (or duplicate) value.\n\n**9. Large amounts of dead/commented-out code \u2014 Low (maintainability)**\nThe entire old `DRAW`/`drawing` implementation is left commented out inside `eye_mag_report()`, plus unused helper functions `left_overs()` and `full_report()` (which `return;` immediately). These should be removed before merge rather than kept as inline history.\n\n**10. Untranslated hardcoded strings \u2014 Low (i18n)**\n```php\n<b>Orders/Next Visit:</b>\n...\n<td>Comments:</td>\n```\nThese should use `xlt()` like the rest of the file.\n\n**11. No input casting for IDs \u2014 Medium (defense in depth)**\n`$pid`, `$encounter`, `$form_id` are taken from `$_REQUEST` with only a truthiness check, never cast with `(int)`/`intval()`. Even though most queries are parameterized, enforcing numeric types would reduce the blast radius of issues like #1 and guard against unexpected types being passed through to file-path construction.\n\n**12. Global, generically-named functions with no guard \u2014 Low**\n`narrative()`, `display_draw_image()`, `report_ACT()`, `left_overs()`, `full_report()` are declared in the global namespace with generic names and no `function_exists()` guard or namespacing. If this file is ever included more than once in a request lifecycle (or another form defines a same-named helper), it will trigger a fatal \"cannot redeclare function\" error.\n\n**13. `left_overs()` references undefined variables \u2014 Low**\n```php\nfunction left_overs() {\n  $data = formFetch($table_name, $id);\n```\n`$table_name` and `$id` are neither parameters nor globals here \u2014 this function is effectively broken if ever called. Since it's unused dead code, low severity, but should be removed rather than left half-working.\n\n**14. Missing trailing newline / stray closing tag at EOF \u2014 Trivial**\nFile ends with `?>` and no trailing newline; per project convention the closing `?>` tag is normally omitted entirely for pure-PHP files to avoid accidental whitespace output."
